      <h2 class="section-title">Frequently Asked <span class="accent">Questions</span></h2>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What is a Hashi puzzle?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">Hashi (also called Bridges or Hashiwokakero) is a drawing-based logic puzzle. Numbered islands sit on a grid; you connect them with horizontal and vertical bridges. Each island's number is the exact number of bridges it needs, up to two bridges may join the same pair, bridges never cross, and the finished puzzle must connect all islands into one network.</div>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Is Hashi the same as Bridges?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">Yes. Bridges is the English name for the puzzle published in Japanese as Hashiwokakero ("build bridges"), usually shortened to Hashi. Same rules, same gameplay — only the name changes.</div>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Are Hashi puzzles good for your brain?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">Yes. Hashi trains spatial reasoning, counting and whole-network planning. Unlike Sudoku's simple elimination, every bridge satisfies two islands at once and the connectivity rule forces you to keep the entire archipelago in your head, giving a genuinely different mental workout.</div>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Is this Hashi puzzle free?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">Yes — the GridPaw Hashi puzzle is completely free to play in your browser. No download, no account, no payment. Pick Easy 8×8, Medium 10×10 or Hard 12×12 and start bridging islands immediately.</div>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Can I play Hashi on mobile?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">Yes. The board is fully touch-friendly: tap an island then its neighbor to place a bridge, or tap the gap between two islands to cycle the count. On desktop you can also use the arrow keys, Enter and the 1/2/0 keys.</div>
      </details>
